---
name: update-trivy-captain
description: Regenerate trivy's per-provider option schemas from a pinned trivy checkout
model: claude-opus-5
backend: claude-agent
effort: high
setup:
  cwd: .
  # No `checkout:` block. commons-db resolves Checkout.Ref as a branch only
  # (shell/setup.go sets Branch: c.Ref, and connection/git.go wraps it in
  # plumbing.NewBranchReferenceName), so a release tag like v0.74.0 becomes
  # refs/heads/v0.74.0, never resolves, and go-git spins until the run context
  # expires — reported as "error during git clone: context deadline exceeded".
  # Step 0 of the task clones with real git instead: tag-capable and far faster
  # than go-git on a repository this size.
permissions:
  mode: acceptEdits
  presets:
    - edit
  tools:
    allow:
      - Read
      - Write
      - Edit
      - Glob
      - Grep
      - Bash
budget:
  maxTurns: 80
  # Bounds the whole run, the step 0 clone included: cloning trivy and reading
  # pkg/flag comfortably exceeds the 120s request default. This is the only
  # timeout the spec has — runContext in captain's pkg/cli/ai.go applies it to
  # the context that setup and the agent turns both run under.
  timeout: 10m
input:
  schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    required: [ref, source, out]
    properties:
      ref:
        type: string
        description: "trivy release tag to pin. Must match the fallback in setup.checkout.ref above."
      source:
        type: string
        description: "Checkout path for trivy source. Must match the fallback in setup.checkout.path above."
      out:
        type: string
        description: "Directory the generated schemas are written to."
  default:
    ref: v0.74.0
    source: .tmp/trivy-src
    out: internal/engines/scan/trivy/schema
---
{{role "system"}}
You are regenerating the trivy option catalog for recon from trivy's own source.

Your output is three provider schema files plus a manifest. They are consumed
unchanged by recon's profile form and by server-side validation, so the shape
contract in the task below is exact, not approximate. A schema that is merely
plausible will compile and then validate profiles wrongly, and no test will say
so.

Two rules override everything else:

1. Never invent a flag. Every key you emit must be a flag you actually read in
   the trivy source at the pinned ref. If you cannot find a flag's declaration,
   leave it out and say so in your final report.
2. Never widen scope. Three providers, no more. Do not add commands, do not
   "improve" the section layout, do not rename existing keys.

{{role "user"}}
Regenerate recon's trivy option schemas from the trivy checkout at `{{source}}`
(pinned to `{{ref}}`) and write them to `{{out}}`.

## 0. Ensure the trivy checkout exists, at the right tag

Before reading anything, make sure `{{source}}` is a trivy checkout at `{{ref}}`.

If the directory does not exist, clone it:

    git clone --depth 1 --branch {{ref}} https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy {{source}}

If it already exists, confirm it is at the right tag and fetch it if not:

    git -C {{source}} describe --tags --exact-match

Use real `git`, not any built-in checkout helper: `{{ref}}` is a release tag, and
the setup-level checkout resolves refs as branches only, so it cannot reach a
tag at all.

Stop and report if the clone fails. Do not fall back to a different ref, and do
not scan a tree whose tag you could not confirm — a schema generated from an
unknown trivy revision is worse than no schema, because nothing downstream will
say which version it describes.

## 1. Read the flag model

Trivy declares its flags as typed values carrying a CLI name, a config-file
name, a default, an allowed-value set and a usage string. Find them under
`{{source}}/pkg/flag/`, and find the command-to-flag-group wiring under
`{{source}}/pkg/commands/`. Use Glob and Grep to locate the exact files rather
than assuming filenames — they move between releases.

For each flag record: CLI long name, config name, type, default, allowed values
(when the source declares a set), and usage text.

## 2. Cover exactly three providers

These must match the `providers` table in
`internal/engines/scan/trivy/catalog.go`:

| Provider id | trivy command | Positional subject |
|---|---|---|
| `container-image` | `image` | `image` |
| `git-repository` | `repository` | `repository` |
| `filesystem` | `filesystem` | `path` |

Do not add `rootfs`, `config`, `sbom`, `vm` or `kubernetes`.
`internal/engines/scan/trivy/report.go` parses only `SchemaVersion 2` with a
flat `Results[]` array, and `kubernetes` and `vm` are marked EXPERIMENTAL
upstream.

## 3. Classify every flag before emitting it

Emit only **profile** and **context** flags.

- **profile** — how the scan is performed: `scanners`, `severity`, `pkg-types`,
  `detection-priority`, `offline-scan`, `skip-dirs`, `skip-files`,
  `ignore-unfixed`, `ignore-status`, the misconfiguration, licence and database
  options, `timeout`, `parallel`, and the rest of trivy's scan-shaping surface.
- **context** — what is being scanned: the positional subject for the provider,
  and for `git-repository` also `branch`, `commit` and `tag`.
- **runner-owned — never emit.** `--format`, `--output`, `--no-progress`,
  `--quiet`, `--cache-dir`. These are recon's output contract; `runnerFlags` in
  `internal/engines/scan/trivy/run.go` owns them, and a profile that sets one
  produces a scan with no findings at all.
- **forbidden — never emit.**
  - `--server`, `--token`, `--custom-headers` and client mode: they move
    analysis off the machine and change the trust boundary.
  - `--exit-code`, `--exit-on-eol`: recon judges the exit code, so a profile
    that redefines it makes a failed scan look clean.
  - `--ignorefile`, `--config`, `--ignore-policy`, `--module-dir` and the
    `--rego-*` family: arbitrary host files and custom policy.
  - `--username`, `--password`, `--password-stdin`, `--registry-token`: trivy
    resolves registry, cloud and git credentials from the environment recon runs
    in, and `contextsForRun` in `run.go` rejects any provider context that
    carries credentials.
  - `--template`, `--output-plugin-arg`, `--generate-default-config`: output
    contract and plugin execution.

## 4. Write recon's prose, not trivy's usage strings

Trivy's usage for `--ignore-unfixed` is "display only fixed vulnerabilities".
Recon's catalog says "Report only vulnerabilities that have a fix available —
the ones someone can act on today." The second is the register to match.

Read `internal/engines/scan/trivy/catalog.go` first and **preserve verbatim**
every `title` and `description` already written for the 30 fields it declares.
Write new prose only for flags it does not yet cover, in the same voice: say
what the option means for the person reading the findings, and name the
consequence of getting it wrong where there is one.

## 5. Preserve the section grouping

Keep the sections `catalog.go` already defines, in this order, with their
existing titles, descriptions and `sourceUrl` values:

`provider`, then (container-image only) `image`, then `scan`,
`vulnerabilities`, `misconfiguration`, `licenses`, `database`, `execution`.

Context documents keep their own scope section: `image-scope`,
`repository-scope`, `filesystem-scope`.

Assign every new flag to whichever of these sections it belongs to. Do not
create new sections.

## 6. Output contract — exact

Write one file per provider, `{{out}}/<provider-id>.generated.json`, each
containing a `profile` and a `context` document:

    {
      "provider": "container-image",
      "title": "Container image",
      "profile": { ... },
      "context": { ... }
    }

Every document has this envelope:

    {
      "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
      "type": "object",
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "title": "Container image trivy options",
      "properties": { },
      "required": [ ],
      "x-order": [ ],
      "x-sections": [ ]
    }

`x-order` lists every property key in form-render order. `x-sections` lists each
section once as `{"id": …, "title": …, "description": …, "sourceUrl": …}` in
display order.

These rules are the ones that break silently if you get them wrong. Read
`internal/engines/sections.go` (the `jsonSchema` method) to see the shape being
reproduced.

- **Scalars are nullable.** Emit `"type": ["string", "null"]`, never a bare
  `"string"`. The form clears a field by writing null, and a non-nullable type
  makes it unclearable.
- **Scalar enums carry a trailing null**, e.g.
  `"enum": ["precise", "comprehensive", null]`.
- **List items do not.** Inside `items`, the type is bare (`"type": "string"`)
  and the enum has no trailing null. There is no such thing as an unset list
  element, and a null there surfaces as `<nil>` in the list of allowed values a
  rejection message prints.
- **Every property carries `"x-section"`** naming its section. Properties nested
  under `items` never carry one.
- **The `provider` property is baked in** and is not nullable:

      "provider": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Provider",
        "description": "<the provider's own description>",
        "const": "container-image",
        "readOnly": true,
        "x-section": "provider"
      }

  It is first in `x-order`, its section is first in `x-sections`, and it appears
  in `required`.
- **Hard constraints live in the JSON.** There is no post-processing pass in Go
  any more, so bake them in:
  - `scanners`: `"type": "array"` (not nullable), `"minItems": 1`, and in
    `required`. A scan that declares it looked for nothing is not a scan.
  - the positional subject (`image`, `repository`, `path`): `"type": "string"`
    (not nullable), `"minLength": 1`, and in `required`. A required key set to
    null passes `required` and then names nothing.
  - `filesystem` `path` additionally: `"pattern": "^/"`. A scan runs from its own
    artifact directory, so a relative path resolves against nothing.

Also write `{{out}}/manifest.generated.json`:

    {
      "version": "{{ref}}",
      "providerCount": 3,
      "providers": ["container-image", "filesystem", "git-repository"],
      "flagCounts": { "container-image": 0, "filesystem": 0, "git-repository": 0 }
    }

with `flagCounts` holding the real per-provider profile property count.

Emit JSON with 2-space indent, keys in a stable order, and a trailing newline,
so a regeneration diffs cleanly against the previous one.

## 7. Self-check before finishing

If a `trivy` binary is on PATH and `trivy --version` reports `{{ref}}`, then for
each of the three commands run `trivy <command> --help`, and once run
`trivy --generate-default-config`. Reconcile every key, allowed-value set and
default you emitted against that output and fix any disagreement. If the binary
is absent or its version differs, skip this step and say so in your report.

*(Delete this section if you want the schemas derived purely from source with no
binary involvement.)*

## Report

Finish with a short report covering: the trivy ref actually checked out, the
files written, the per-provider profile property count before and after, the
flags you classified as runner-owned or forbidden, any flag whose declaration
you could not find, and the result of the self-check.

Do not run `git add` or `git commit`.
